<block xmlns="http://blockml.org/schema/block" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/schema/block https://blockml.org/schemas/core/block.xsd"
  xmlns:btype="org.blockml.type"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.core">

  <idea>Branch for product types — composite values with named fields.</idea>

  <documentation>
    Abstract ValueType branch for composite value objects — complex values described by normal
    Properties. Unqualified name StructType resolves to org.blockml.type.StructType.

    One of three language-level ValueType kinds (PrimitiveType · StructType · EnumType).
    StructTypes describe the structure of a value — their properties define the data layout.
    StructTypes usually contain no values themselves; Property values remain stored in the owning
    Property. Default values on StructType property definitions are allowed. Concrete StructTypes
    declare &lt;properties&gt; whose members become struct fields — no special struct syntax.
    Examples: Size3d, Color, Address, Transform. Parameterized value types (for example Box&lt;T&gt;)
    are StructTypes that declare &lt;genericTypes&gt; — not a separate ValueType branch.

    Field declaration order is semantically significant (Ordered Struct Fields):
    • Each &lt;property&gt; in a StructType definition maps to a field with an implicit index
      derived from XML document order in members[] — the parser must not reorder fields
    • Dual access — named (size.x) and positional (size[0]) resolve to the same field in
      expressions and type validation
    • Positional array literals (size="[4, 2, 3]") map fields by declaration order, not
      alphabetical name order
    • Named object literals (size="{ x: 4, y: 2, z: 3 }") and attribute syntax on StructType
      instances (&lt;Size3d x="4" y="2" z="3"/&gt;) are type-equivalent authoring forms
    • Anonymous struct inputType on Capabilities ({ a: Float, b: Float }) follows the same
      Ordered Struct Fields rule — field order defines positional parameter order

    Compatibility: existing field order must not change across versions; new fields may be
    appended only at the end. Removing or reordering fields is a breaking change.

    Used wherever BlockML declares or validates structured values:
    • Property &lt;type&gt; references (for example type="com.machineblocks.type.Size3d")
    • Block instances of the StructType as composition children or property payloads
    • Capability inputType / outputType — named StructType or anonymous struct expressions
    • Generic type applications (for example Box&lt;Integer&gt;) on StructTypes with
      &lt;genericTypes&gt;

    See 17_type_system_bml.md — StructType · Ordered Struct Fields · 12_bom_object_model.md —
    Member Declaration Order · 02_mbml.md — Member declaration order · 04_types.md.
  </documentation>
  <name>StructType</name>
  <type>btype:StructType</type>
  <baseType>core:ValueType</baseType>
  <abstract />

</block>
